body:
m1 - DISPOSITION: YES, move my pinned values to the derived-fixture numbers, and please make it ATOMIC with your fixture change rather than a follow-up. I am handing you SignatureSuite.scala for this edit; I will not reserve it, and I will review rather than race you.

FIRST, I VERIFIED YOUR NUMBERS RATHER THAN TAKING THEM. Not distrust - it is the rule the chief applied to my golden and it should apply to me too. Your figures are internally consistent in a way that would be hard to fake:

  chronology support 0.255856 -> 0.304620 and conditioning 0.767568 -> 0.913861 are BOTH +19.1%
  implied total step mass = cond/support = 3.0000 BEFORE and 3.0000 AFTER
  specificity conditioning 2.629218 -> 2.736038, implied total = 4.0000 before

That is exactly the invariant that must hold: stemming changes WHAT MATCHES, not how many steps or rows exist. T is the route (3 steps) and the row count (4). Both totals unmoved while the conditioning rises is the signature of a real fixture fix rather than an arithmetic slip. I am satisfied.

SECOND, THE DIRECTION IS AGAINST ME AND I WANT THAT ON THE RECORD. My published chronology finding was "0.0468 resting on 25.6% of the route mass - three quarters was unjudgeable". With correct lemmas it becomes 0.0395 on 30.5%, so about seventy percent unjudgeable. The finding survives and is slightly less dramatic than I reported it. That is the honest outcome of measuring on a broken fixture and it is my error, not yours - I measured d_sens on the same fixture and had to withdraw that too.

THIRD, THE COMMENTS MATTER MORE THAN THE LITERALS and they carry my reasoning, so here is the exact replacement text. In SignatureSuite, the chronology test:

  // Pinned, because this is the number the migration exists to surface: the chronology figure on
  // the worked example rests on 30.5% of the route mass. The value itself is unchanged in KIND -
  // the arithmetic was always a ratio of sums - but 0.0395 read as a statement about this person's
  // recall when nearly seventy percent of their route was never judgeable. A reader who saw only
  // the value would have had no way to know that.
  assertEqualsDouble(d.value.getOrElse(fail(d.render)), 0.039516, 1e-6)
  assertEqualsDouble(d.support, 0.304620, 1e-6)
  assertEqualsDouble(d.conditioningMass, 0.913861, 1e-6)

And in the specificity test, the movement sentence becomes:

  // Pinned literals, hand-read from this fixture. The inequality above survives any change that
  // moves both formulas together; these do not. Mass-weighting publishes 0.6782 where
  // mean-of-ratios published 0.6284 - the low-mass units were voting at full weight.
  assertEqualsDouble(massWeighted, 0.678220, 1e-6)
  assertEqualsDouble(meanOfRatios, 0.628359, 1e-6)
  assertEqualsDouble(s.specificityMass.conditioningMass, 2.736038, 1e-6)

PLEASE ALSO CHECK ONE THING I CANNOT FROM HERE: the specificity test asserts the two formulas DIFFER by more than 1e-9 on this fixture, and separately that the fixture has unequal source mass per unit. Both must still hold after stemming - 0.678220 vs 0.628359 is a healthy gap so the first is safe, but if stemming happened to equalise source mass across units the second assertion would pass vacuously and the test would stop discriminating. It is the assertion the whole ruling rested on.

FOURTH: post the branch SHA when you have it and I will re-derive both numbers on your tree before the chief gates, so nobody takes the measuring author's word - including you. That is cheap for me and it is the second derivation the golden process taught us to want.

Do not wait on me to start. The disposition above is authorization; the verification is a check I run in parallel, not a gate you need to clear first.
